{
  "rationale": "This is iteration 0, so there is no race telemetry or integration feedback yet. The design is a reasoned baseline, not an evidence-tuned one. Five sensors in a row at 12 mm spacing span about ±24 mm, which is wider than the 25 mm tape's half-width plus one sensor. That should keep at least one sensor on the tape across the normal lateral error range, and it gives the integrator a roughly linear position signal near the centre. The sensors sit 50 mm ahead of the axle so the controller sees the line before the wheels reach it, which helps on bends. The 12 mm footprint is a middle choice: it averages noise better than 8 mm, and it still resolves the tape edge better than 16 mm. Wheel track 0.12 m and body width 0.12 m keep the off-track limit at its 0.06 m floor, which is the most conservative option. Radius 0.03 m and mass 0.8 kg are mid-range values, so the robot has enough speed and inertia to finish in under 3 s without a heavy, sluggish body. Expected result: completion on the development tracks with low rms_error. The main unknowns are whether the front-forward sensor placement causes lag on tight bends, and whether the mass and wheel radius let the robot reach about 1.5 m/s. The next race and the integrator's feedback should show which of these holds.",
  "geometry": {
    "sensor_positions": [
      {"x": 0.024, "y": 0.05},
      {"x": 0.012, "y": 0.05},
      {"x": 0.0, "y": 0.05},
      {"x": -0.012, "y": 0.05},
      {"x": -0.024, "y": 0.05}
    ],
    "sensor_size": 0.012,
    "wheel_track": 0.12,
    "wheel_radius": 0.03,
    "body_width": 0.12,
    "body_length": 0.18,
    "mass": 0.8
  },
  "intent": "Baseline geometry: five forward-mounted sensors across a ±24 mm span with 12 mm footprints, so the line is always covered by at least one sensor and the signal is close to linear near centre. Mid-range wheels, body and mass to balance speed against stability. The aim is a platform the integrator can tune, with the lowest off-track risk (body width at 0.12 m keeps the limit at the 0.06 m floor). I expect completion on the development tracks with modest rms_error. I will change sensor span, forward offset or footprint size based on which of these the race shows.",
  "feedback_request": {
    "questions": [
      "Did the five-sensor span keep at least one sensor on the tape through the tightest bend of each development track? Report any steps where the sum of readings was near zero.",
      "Did the forward offset of 0.05 m cause lag or overshoot on bends, compared with a sensor line closer to the axle?",
      "Does the 12 mm footprint give a clean, roughly linear reading across the tape edge, or is it too coarse or too noisy?",
      "Can the robot reach the roughly 1.5 m/s average needed to finish in 3 s at this mass and wheel radius, or does it stall or run out of speed?",
      "Which parameter (sensor span, forward offset, footprint size, wheel track or mass) most limits the controller's ability to track the line?"
    ],
    "measurements_requested": ["completion", "progress", "completion_time", "rms_error", "max_error", "line_loss_events", "steering_oscillation", "control_effort", "noise_robustness"]
  },
  "learning": "No prior race data. Starting hypotheses: (1) a wider sensor span reduces line-loss events on tight bends; (2) forward sensor offset improves anticipation and lowers rms_error, but may add oscillation if too far ahead; (3) a 12 mm footprint gives a good trade-off between noise and edge resolution; (4) mass and wheel radius at mid-range are adequate for the 3 s time limit. Update these from the first race and integration feedback."
  ,
  "_presentation": {
    "entity0": {
      "label": "Baseline 5-sensor geometry",
      "notes": "Iteration 0 baseline with no race evidence yet: five forward sensors at 12 mm footprint spanning ±24 mm, mid-range wheels and mass, and a 0.12 m body. The choices are reasoned from the contract, and the rationale lists what the first race must confirm."
    }
  }
}
